## Generating the First N Primes

### The Algorithm

A number is prime if it's greater than 1 and has no divisors other than 1 and itself. We generate primes by checking each number against all previously found primes.

```xiom
fn generate_primes(n: Int) -> Vec[Int] {
  let mut primes: Vec[Int] = Vec[Int].new();
  let mut candidate = 2;
  while primes.len() < n {
    let mut is_prime = true;
    let mut i = 0;
    while i < primes.len() {
      let p = primes.get(i).unwrap();
      if p * p > candidate { break; };
      if candidate % p == 0 {
        is_prime = false;
        break;
      };
      i += 1;
    };
    if is_prime {
      primes.push(candidate);
    };
    candidate += 1;
  };
  primes
}
```

### Optimization: Only Check Up To sqrt(n)

If `p * p > candidate`, no larger prime can divide candidate. This dramatically reduces checks for large numbers.

### Printing the Results

```xiom
fn main() -> Int {
  let primes = generate_primes(20);
  let mut i = 0;
  while i < primes.len() {
    io.println(primes.get(i).unwrap());
    i += 1;
  };
  return 0;
}
```

